(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-14"] = {"7.1":{"s":"A random variable turns each outcome of a chance experiment into a real number: a rule defined on the sample space, so its value is not known until the experiment is done. For example, the sum of two dice or the number of heads in some tosses. Studying the probabilities of its values (its distribution) lets us describe chance experiments with numbers.","f":[{"x":"P(X = xᵢ) = pᵢ , pᵢ ≥ 0","n":"Probability mass function. For a discrete random variable X."},{"x":"f(x) ≥ 0 , ∫ over all x of f(x) dx = 1","n":"Probability density function. Continuous random variable."}],"w":["A random variable is a function on the sample space; one outcome gives exactly one value."],"x":["In a chance experiment, we often care about a number that comes from the outcome, and not the outcome itself. When three coins are tossed, we may only ask how many heads there are. A random variable is a rule that gives one real number to each outcome in the sample space. It is written with a capital letter, like X.","For example, throw two dice. The sample space has 36 outcomes. Let X be the sum of the two numbers. Then the outcome (3, 4) gives X = 7, and the outcome (6, 6) gives X = 12. The value of X is not known until the experiment is done, but the possible values and their probabilities are known.","We use a random variable to describe an experiment with numbers. The probabilities of its values are called its probability distribution. This lets us find an average value and a spread, and compare different experiments.","Common mistake: thinking that a random variable is a variable that can take any value at random. It is a function on the sample space, and one outcome gives exactly one value."],"e":"Toss a coin twice, and let X be the number of heads. The outcomes HH, HT, TH and TT give X = 2, 1, 1 and 0. So X takes the values 0, 1 and 2."},"7.2":{"s":"A discrete random variable takes only a finite or countable list of values (the number of heads, the sum on dice). A continuous random variable takes any value in an interval of real numbers, found by measurement (a time or a length). The two kinds have different kinds of probability distribution.","f":[{"x":"P(X = xᵢ) = pᵢ , pᵢ ≥ 0","n":"Probability mass function. For a discrete random variable X."},{"x":"f(x) ≥ 0 , ∫ over all x of f(x) dx = 1","n":"Probability density function. Continuous random variable."}],"w":["Counting gives a discrete variable; measuring gives a continuous one."],"x":["A random variable is discrete if it takes a finite number of values, or a countable list of values, such as 0, 1, 2, 3 and so on. Discrete variables come from counting. Examples: the number of heads in 5 tosses, the number of defective bulbs in a box, the sum on two dice.","A random variable is continuous if it can take any value in an interval of real numbers. Continuous variables come from measuring. Examples: the time that a bulb works, the height of a student, the length of a leaf, the weight of a bag.","The two kinds have different kinds of probability distribution. For a discrete variable, we give the probability of each value. For a continuous variable, the probability of one exact value is zero, so we give a density function and find the probability of an interval from an area.","A quick test: if you can list the values one after another, it is discrete. If between any two values there is another possible value, it is continuous. Common mistake: calling a measured quantity discrete because it is rounded to whole units."],"e":"Classify: the number of cars that pass a gate in an hour is discrete. The time between two cars is continuous. The number of students absent today is discrete. The temperature in a room is continuous."},"7.3":{"s":"The probability distribution of a discrete random variable lists its values x with their probabilities p; each p is at least 0 and the probabilities add to 1. This is the probability mass function, and the cumulative distribution function F(x) adds the probabilities up to x. The expected value (mean) is the sum of each value times its probability, and the variance measures the spread around it; the standard deviation is its square root.","f":[{"x":"p_i = P[X = x_i] >= 0 and Σp_i = 1","n":"Conditions for a probability distribution (p.m.f.).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.223"},{"x":"F(x) = P[X <= x] = Σp_i over all x_i <= x","n":"Cumulative distribution function of a discrete variable.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.224"},{"x":"E(X) = Σx_i*p_i","n":"Expected value (mean) of X.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.229"},{"x":"Var(X) = Σ(x_i - mu)^2*p_i","n":"Variance; mu = E(X). The standard deviation is the square root of Var(X).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.229"},{"x":"Var(X) = E(X^2) - (E(X))^2","n":"Simplified form; E(X^2) = Σx_i^2*p_i.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.229"}],"w":["If your probabilities do not add up to 1, find the unknown constant first.","Var(X) = E(X²) − [E(X)]²: square the mean AFTER averaging, not before."],"x":["The probability distribution of a discrete random variable X lists each possible value xᵢ with its probability pᵢ = P(X = xᵢ). Two conditions must hold: every pᵢ is at least 0, and the sum of all the pᵢ is 1. If one probability is given in terms of an unknown constant, use the sum 1 to find the constant first.","The cumulative distribution function is F(x) = P(X ≤ x), the sum of the pᵢ for all xᵢ up to x. It is a step function that never decreases, and it ends at 1.","The mean, or expected value, is E(X) = Σ xᵢpᵢ. It is the long-run average. The variance measures the spread around the mean: Var(X) = Σ(xᵢ − μ)²pᵢ. A shorter way is Var(X) = E(X²) − [E(X)]², where E(X²) = Σ xᵢ²pᵢ. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance. For constants a and b, E(aX + b) = aE(X) + b and Var(aX + b) = a²Var(X).","Common mistakes: squaring the mean before averaging, which means that you find E(X)² wrongly, and getting a negative variance. A variance cannot be negative, so if you get one, check your work."],"e":"X takes the values 1, 2, 3, 4 with probabilities 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4. E(X) = 0.1 + 0.4 + 0.9 + 1.6 = 3.0. E(X²) = 0.1 + 0.8 + 2.7 + 6.4 = 10. Var(X) = 10 − 9 = 1, and the standard deviation is 1."},"7.4":{"s":"For a continuous random variable the probability of any single value is zero, so probabilities come from areas: a probability density function f is never negative, the total area under it is 1, and the probability of an interval is the integral of f over it. The cumulative distribution function F(x) is the integral of f from the lower end of the range up to x, and it never decreases.","f":[{"x":"f(x) >= 0 and ∫_S f(x) dx = 1","n":"Conditions for a probability density function on the support S.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.4, p.234"},{"x":"P[X in A] = ∫_A f(x) dx","n":"Probability that X lies in the interval A.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.4, p.234"},{"x":"F(x) = ∫_a^x f(t) dt","n":"Cumulative distribution function for a < x < b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.4, p.234"}],"w":["f(x) is a height, not a probability: it can exceed 1, only areas are probabilities."],"x":["For a continuous random variable, the probability of any single exact value is zero. So we use a probability density function f(x). It has two properties: f(x) is never negative, and the total area under the graph is 1, that is, the integral of f over the whole range is 1. The probability that X is in an interval is the area under the curve over that interval: P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = the integral of f(x) from a to b.","The height f(x) is not a probability. It is a density, and it can be more than 1. Only the area is a probability. Because single points have no area, P(X = a) = 0, and it does not matter whether the end points of an interval are included.","The cumulative distribution function is F(x) = P(X ≤ x), the integral of f(t) from the lower end of the range to x. It never decreases, it is 0 at the left end and 1 at the right end, and f(x) = F′(x). The mean is the integral of x f(x) dx over the range.","To find an unknown constant in f, put the total area equal to 1. Common mistake: finding the probability by putting x in f(x) directly, which gives the height, not the probability."],"e":"f(x) = kx² on 0 to 3. The area is k × 27/3 = 9k = 1, so k = 1/9. Then P(1 ≤ X ≤ 2) = (8 − 1)/27 = 7/27, about 0.259."}};
